# Greeting and weekly report
{100}{}{You're back. Sit down if you've got the time. The Gutter doesn't wait for you, but it counts your money.}
{101}{}{Last week we had }
{102}{}{ customers. After costs we cleared $}
{103}{}{. There's $}
{104}{}{ in the till.}

{110}{}{Give me what's in the till.}
{111}{}{Let's talk prices.}
{112}{}{Let's talk about what our people get.}
{113}{}{How are our people holding up?}
{114}{}{You said there's a problem.}
{115}{}{Later.}
{116}{}{Money?}
{117}{}{Let's talk about the house. What could we build?}
{118}{}{Let's talk about our people.}

# Payout
{120}{}{Here. $}
{121}{}{. Count it if you like. I counted it twice.}
{122}{}{There's nothing. The week cost more than it brought in. It happens. In the Den it happens a lot.}
{123}{}{Good. Go on.}
{124}{}{That's all.}

# Prices
{130}{}{The Den is poor, sweetheart. Charge too much here and the rooms go empty. Charge too little and you get the kind of customers nobody wants.}
{131}{}{Cheap. Quantity over quality.}
{132}{}{The usual prices.}
{133}{}{Charge more. We're better than the rest.}
{134}{}{Leave it as it is.}
{135}{}{Right now we take whatever people can still scrape together.}
{136}{}{Right now we charge the usual.}
{137}{}{Right now we charge more than the others.}
{138}{}{Right now we charge prices nobody in the Den can pay.}
{139}{}{As you like. Starting next week.}

# Staff share
{140}{}{Right now our people keep }
{141}{}{ percent of what they bring in. The rest belongs to the house. What do you want to change?}
{142}{}{A quarter is enough. They should be glad to have a roof.}
{143}{}{The usual. A third.}
{144}{}{Almost half. Whoever works well should live well.}
{145}{}{You can do that. Once. Then the good ones leave, and you're left with the ones who have nowhere else to go. You won't think about them after that, will you?}
{146}{}{That's how it's done in the Den. Nobody gets rich, nobody starves.}
{147}{}{Either you're smarter than the others, or you haven't been here long enough. They'll thank you for it. In their own way.}
{148}{}{Leave everything as it is.}

# Morale
{150}{}{They're laughing again. Not in front of the johns, they always do that. With each other.}
{151}{}{They work. They complain. That's a good sign, as long as they still do.}
{152}{}{They don't talk to me anymore. That's not a good sign.}
{153}{}{One of them ran off last night. She took nothing, not even her shoes. Think about what that means.}

# Crisis: the drug
{160}{}{One of ours is hooked on Jet. A week now. She's already stealing from the till, and soon she'll be stealing from the johns' pockets. Then she's dead, or we are.}
{161}{}{[Doctor] I'll look at her myself.}
{162}{}{Here's $200. Get her what she needs.}
{163}{}{Pay her in Jet instead of wages. At least she won't run.}
{164}{}{Throw her out.}
{165}{}{She screamed for three days. Now she's sleeping. Thank you. Nobody here forgets a thing like that.}
{166}{}{I'll take her to someone who knows about these things. I can't promise anything.}
{167}{}{I won't tell her that. You tell her yourself. And then watch how the others look at you afterward.}
{168}{}{She'll end up in the gutter. The real one. But fine, it's your house.}

# Other crises
{170}{}{Something happened that I can't handle alone.}
{171}{}{Here's $300. Take care of it.}
{172}{}{I'll deal with it later.}
{173}{}{Money doesn't fix everything. It fixes this.}
{181}{}{Soldiers in black armor. Drunk. They won't leave, and none of us can make them.}
{182}{}{Someone's hooked on Jet.}
{183}{}{They come and search the house. Not for the first time.}
{184}{}{A john beat one of ours half to death. He says he'll be back.}
{185}{}{Something's going around. Two are already down.}
{186}{}{Money's missing from the till. Not much. Not yet.}
{187}{}{Two are gone. Just gone.}

